  return new;
end;
$$;

create trigger businesses_seed_accounts
after insert on public.businesses
for each row execute function private.on_business_created();
```

**Does every business type get the same seed, including `tiket`?** Yes — the trigger fires `after insert on public.businesses for each row`, unconditionally, regardless of `business_type`. There is no `business_type`-conditional branching anywhere in `seed_default_accounts` or the trigger. I confirmed via grep across every migration (`Pendapatan Tiket|pendapatan_tiket|4-002|tiket` search against `supabase/migrations/`) that **no ticket-specific account currently exists** — the only files matching `tiket` are the three ticket-venue migrations themselves and the admin-panel dashboard query (which just counts `ticket_transactions` rows for stats, unrelated to COA). So **you will need to add a new revenue account** (e.g. code `4-002 Pendapatan Tiket`) if you want ticket revenue booked separately from `4-001 Pendapatan Penjualan` — currently there is no such account, and reusing `4-001` for both product sales and ticket sales is the only option without a COA migration.

Confirmed account codes relevant to your integration:
- **Kas & Bank**: `1-001` (aset, debit, is_system=true) — confirmed.
- **PPN/tax liability**: `2-200 PPN Keluaran (Utang Pajak)` (kewajiban, kredit, is_system=true) — confirmed, matches memory.
- **Persediaan**: `1-200` (aset, debit) — used for HPP contra in fnb/retail; not directly relevant to tickets since there's no ticket inventory.

---

## 5. Does `ticket_transactions`/`ticket_categories` support PPN/tax?

**Yes**, fully, mirroring fnb/retail's exact pattern. From the `ticket_transactions` table DDL (`20260706200000_ticket_venue.sql`, lines 102–124):

```sql
create table public.ticket_transactions (
  id uuid primary key default gen_random_uuid(),
  business_id uuid not null references public.businesses (id) on delete cascade,
  shift_id uuid references public.shifts (id) on delete set null,
  cashier_id uuid not null references public.cashiers (id),
  member_id uuid references public.members (id) on delete set null,
  invoice_number text not null,
  date timestamptz not null default now(),
  is_holiday boolean not null default false,
  subtotal numeric(12, 2) not null default 0,
  service numeric(12, 2) not null default 0,
  tax numeric(12, 2) not null default 0,
  total numeric(12, 2) not null default 0,
  payment_method text not null,
  received numeric(12, 2),
  change numeric(12, 2) not null default 0,
  voided boolean not null default false,
  voided_at timestamptz,
  void_reason text,
  voided_by uuid references public.cashiers (id),
  constraint ticket_transactions_business_id_invoice_number_key
    unique (business_id, invoice_number)
);
```

`subtotal`, `service`, `tax`, `total` columns exist, and inside `checkout_ticket_transaction` they're computed identically to `checkout_transaction`:

```sql
  if v_business.service_enabled then
    v_service := round(v_subtotal * v_business.service_rate / 100);
  end if;

  if v_business.tax_enabled then
    v_tax := round((v_subtotal + v_service) * v_business.tax_rate / 100);
  end if;

  v_total := v_subtotal + v_service + v_tax;
```

`v_business` is populated from `select b.tax_enabled, b.tax_rate, b.service_enabled, b.service_rate from public.businesses b` — the same `businesses` table columns fnb/retail uses, and the migration comment (line 6, `20260706200000_ticket_venue.sql`) explicitly says: *"Reuse cashiers/shifts/pengaturan pajak-service yang sudah ada di businesses — tidak ada konsep baru di sana"* ("Reuse the existing cashiers/shifts/tax-service settings on businesses — no new concept there"). So **you should mirror the exact same PPN-split pattern** as `checkout_transaction`/`void_transaction` §3 above: debit Kas & Bank for `v_total`, credit revenue account for `subtotal + service`, credit `2-200` for `tax` (conditionally, if `tax > 0`).

**`ticket_categories`** (lines 21–32) has no tax-related columns — tax/service is business-level, not per-category, same as fnb/retail.

---

## 6. Cost basis for tickets (HPP/COGS)

**None exists, and none is planned.** Confirmed by reading the full `ticket_categories`, `ticket_serials`, and `ticket_transactions` schemas — no `cost` column anywhere in any of them, and no `total_cost`/`gross_profit` columns on `ticket_transactions` (contrast with `public.transactions` which has `total_cost numeric(12,2)` and `gross_profit numeric(12,2)`, used to drive `v_total_cost` in `checkout_transaction`).

The migration's own top-of-file comment makes this an explicit design decision (`20260706200000_ticket_venue.sql`, lines 97–100):

> "Ticket transactions (header) ... Mirip public.transactions tapi untuk penjualan tiket — sengaja dipisah (bukan union ke transactions) karena **tidak ada konsep HPP/COGS untuk tiket masuk**, dan halaman laporan yang sudah ada tetap scoped ke transactions."

("Similar to public.transactions but for ticket sales — deliberately kept separate (not unioned into transactions) because **there's no HPP/COGS concept for entry tickets**, and existing report pages stay scoped to transactions.")

So your journal entry for a ticket sale should be **revenue-only, no HPP/Persediaan lines, ever** — there's no `v_total_cost`-equivalent variable to compute or gate on. The entry is simply: debit `1-001 Kas & Bank` for `v_total`, credit revenue account for `v_subtotal + v_service`, and conditionally credit `2-200 PPN Keluaran` for `v_tax` if tax is enabled — a 2-or-3-line entry, full stop, following the exact shape of the *first half* of `checkout_transaction`'s journal block (§3) but omitting the HPP/Persediaan `if v_total_cost > 0` block entirely since that condition can never be true for tickets.

---

## Summary of what a new migration needs to do

1. Add a new `perform private.post_journal(...)` block inside `checkout_ticket_transaction`, right before `return query select v_transaction_id, v_invoice_number;` — gated on `v_total > 0` (or more precisely, whenever the 2-line array is non-empty), using `'penjualan'` as source (or a new `'tiket'` value if you extend the `journal_entries_source_check` constraint) and `v_transaction_id` as `source_id`.
2. Add the mirrored reversal block inside `void_ticket_transaction` — but first widen its header `select t.id, t.voided into v_tx ...` to also pull `t.total, t.subtotal, t.service, t.tax, t.invoice_number` (there's no `total_cost` to pull, unlike fnb/retail's void).
3. Decide whether to reuse `4-001 Pendapatan Penjualan` or add a new `4-002 Pendapatan Tiket` account to `seed_default_accounts` (currently no ticket-specific account exists; adding one means also backfilling existing `tiket`-type businesses the same way `20260710140000_ppn_liability.sql` and `20260710150000_fixed_assets_depreciation.sql` did with their `do $$ ... perform private.seed_default_accounts(b.id); ... $$` backfill blocks).
4. No HPP/Persediaan lines are needed or possible — tickets have zero cost-basis columns anywhere in the schema.

